WEBVTT

00:00:00.735 --> 00:00:05.690
Vision keeps applying one map again and again:
a layer repeated through a network,

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:10.678
an update repeated until it settles, a
covariance matrix in PCA.

00:00:10.698 --> 00:00:12.360
Where do the vectors end up?

00:00:12.485 --> 00:00:17.560
Shrink it to the smallest case: one matrix, A,
with rows two, one, and one, two.

00:00:17.935 --> 00:00:21.652
Here are sixteen arrows of length one,
pointing every way.

00:00:21.672 --> 00:00:23.640
Let A act on all of them at once.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.865
Most arrows turn as they stretch.

00:00:25.885 --> 00:00:28.060
But two directions never turn.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:33.538
Along one, one, A gives three, three: the same
direction, three times as long.

00:00:33.558 --> 00:00:36.950
Along one, minus one, A gives it back
unchanged.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.788
These are the eigenvectors, and their stretch
factors,

00:00:39.808 --> 00:00:42.345
three and one, are the eigenvalues.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.730
The circle of tips became an ellipse,

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.100
and its axes lie exactly along the
eigenvectors.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:49.758
Now predict.

00:00:49.778 --> 00:00:52.082
Start almost along one, minus one:

00:00:52.103 --> 00:00:55.033
one tenth of the orange direction, plus one of
the green.

00:00:55.053 --> 00:00:56.645
Apply A ten times.

00:00:56.665 --> 00:00:58.108
Which way does it point?

00:00:58.128 --> 00:00:59.790
Pause and decide.

00:01:02.555 --> 00:01:03.823
Watch it swing.

00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:07.648
Each step triples the orange part and leaves
the green part alone.

00:01:07.668 --> 00:01:12.435
After ten steps, orange is nearly six
thousand, and green is still one.

00:01:12.455 --> 00:01:17.510
So the arrow ends along one, one, the
direction with the largest eigenvalue.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.655
This is power iteration.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.193
Each eigenvalue sets a fate.

00:01:22.212 --> 00:01:25.393
Ten steps of one and a half grow to almost
fifty eight.

00:01:25.413 --> 00:01:28.055
Ten steps of one half shrink to a thousandth.

00:01:28.075 --> 00:01:31.155
And minus one flips the arrow every step.

00:01:31.175 --> 00:01:34.030
Deep networks feel this: stack many layers,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:38.375
and signals explode or vanish unless each
stretch stays near one.

00:01:38.695 --> 00:01:40.063
Back to images.

00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:43.100
Pair every pixel of this photo with its
neighbour on the right,

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.075
and plot one dot per pair.

00:01:45.095 --> 00:01:48.945
Neighbours look alike, so the cloud stretches
along one, one.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.895
Power iteration on its covariance finds the
top eigenvector,

00:01:52.915 --> 00:01:55.470
almost exactly one, one.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.165
That is the first principal direction of PCA,
in Lesson fifteen.

00:02:00.745 --> 00:02:02.425
So remember three things.

00:02:02.445 --> 00:02:06.125
An eigenvector only stretches: A v equals
lambda v.

00:02:06.145 --> 00:02:10.850
Repeating a map multiplies each eigen part by
its own eigenvalue.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:14.145
So the largest eigenvalue, in size, wins.

